# Day 02 – Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) Promotion

## 🎯 Purpose
Transform a standalone server into a Forest Root Domain Controller to centralize management of users, computers, and security policies across the network.

---

## 🔑 Key Concepts & Terms

* Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS): A distributed directory service that stores information about network resources and provides centralized authentication.
* Domain Controller (DC): A server hosting the Active Directory database (ntds.dit) that authenticates users and enforces security policies.
* Forest: The highest-level organizational container in Active Directory. A forest contains one or more domains that share a single schema and trust relationships.
* Forest Root Domain: The very first domain created in an Active Directory forest topology (e.g., corp.local).
* DSRM (Directory Services Restore Mode): A specialized offline boot mode used to perform database repair or backup restoration on a DC.
* Kerberos: The highly secure network authentication protocol used by Active Directory for logins.

---

## 🏗️ Architecture vs Standalone

```text
STANDALONE WORKGROUP (No AD)
+-------------+      +-------------+
| Workstation |      | File Server |
| Local User  |      | Local User  |
+-------------+      +-------------+
 * User must have local accounts on EVERY machine.
 * Zero centralized management.

ACTIVE DIRECTORY DOMAIN
                     +-----------------------+
                     |  Domain Controller    |
                     |       (DC01)          |
                     |  Central Database     |
                     +-----------+-----------+
                                 |
         +-----------------------+-----------------------+
         |                                               |
+--------v----+                                 +--------v----+
| Workstation |                                 | File Server |
| Domain User |                                 | Domain User |
+-------------+                                 +-------------+
 * User authenticates ONCE against DC01.
 * Access granted centrally across all domain resources.
```

